Please read the following research paper abstract and technical details carefully, then answer the specific questions that follow.\n\nPAPER: "Efficient Multi-Modal Learning with Adaptive Cross-Attention Mechanisms"\n\nABSTRACT:\nModern transformer-based models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across various tasks, yet their computational costs remain prohibitive for real-world deployment, especially in multi-modal scenarios where diverse input streams (text, image, audio) must be processed simultaneously. This paper proposes AdaptiveCA, a novel cross-attention mechanism that dynamically adjusts its computational budget based on input complexity. Our approach reduces memory footprint by 42% while maintaining performance within 2% of full-model baselines on standard benchmarks.\n\nMETHODOLOGY:\nThe core innovation involves three components:\n\n1. Complexity Scorer: A lightweight module that assesses the semantic complexity of each input stream in real-time. It uses a 4-layer MLP with ReLU activations, processing embeddings in 256-dimensional space. The scorer outputs a continuous value between 0 and 1, where values closer to 1 indicate higher complexity.\n\n2. Dynamic Routing Module: Based on complexity scores, this module decides which computation paths to activate. Paths marked as "critical" always execute fully. "Standard" paths reduce intermediate dimensions by 50%, while "lightweight" paths use only 25% of the original computation budget.\n\n3. Gradient-aware Cache: A specialized memory management system that prioritizes storing activations for high-gradient regions. This reduces stored activations by approximately 60% compared to standard caching strategies.\n\nEXPERIMENTAL SETUP:\nWe evaluated AdaptiveCA on four benchmark datasets:\n- ImageNet-Text (500K samples): Multi-modal classification combining 256x256 images with 128-token descriptions\n- AudioBERT (1.2M samples): Audio-text pairs with durations up to 30 seconds\n- COCO-Captioning (330K samples): Standard image captioning benchmark\n- Kinetics-400 (306K samples): Video understanding with temporal context\n\nHARDWARE:\nAll experiments were conducted on a single A100 GPU (40GB memory) with batch sizes of 32 for image tasks and 8 for video/audio tasks.\n\nRESULTS:\n[Performance Table]\nTask | Baseline | AdaptiveCA | Memory Reduction | Latency\nImageNet-Text | 94.2% | 93.1% | 38% | 35% faste

